        "\n",
        "## Applied FEM: The Cow\n",
        "\n",
        "In industry, the finite element method is routinely used to identify **stress\n",
        "concentrations** and **structural failure zones** before a part is manufactured.\n",
        "\n",
        "Classically you'd analyze the stress distribution on a simple geometry like:\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n",
        "![cans under load over time](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Cans_Knockdown_Finite_Element_Analysis.gif){group=\"fem-example\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "![a manufactured part during usage](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Strukturanalyse_%28FEM-Simulation%29.jpg?_=20130829104734){group=\"fem-example\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "![airflow around a beam](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Featool-multiphysics-matlab-fem-and-cfd-gui-toolbox.png/960px-Featool-multiphysics-matlab-fem-and-cfd-gui-toolbox.png?_=20180328053533){group=\"fem-example\"}\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "We wish to stay closer to acutal, real life applications though. Everyone knows has done this before: you take a cow and you compress it by applying a load on it (in engineering, this is called a \"cowpression\", like \"cow\" and \"compression\").\n",
        "\n",
        "For this classic example in engineering (analogour to the aerodynamic analysis of a cow), we will solve the Poisson equation on a cow mesh. The solution $u$ will represent the stress distribution on the cow, and the source term $f$ will represent the applied load.\n",
        "\n",
        "{{< video https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VyxMZ2vS3dI >}}"
